<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!-- res/mipmap-anydpi-v26/ic_launcher.xml (and ic_launcher_round.xml, identical).
     minSdk 31 means adaptive-only is fine — no legacy square PNG mipmaps needed.

     The foreground is the site's own maskable icon (🗾 on #fdf6e3), inset by
     tools/gen-launcher-icon.py so the artwork lands on 47.5% of the 108dp canvas (2.1.0,
     bug A-2). The earlier note here said the source's 62.5% artwork "already sits inside
     the 66.7% an adaptive icon guarantees is visible, so it is used at full canvas size" —
     that read a FLOOR as a target. Drawn full size the glyph filled ~82% of the visible
     circle on both launchers measured and Pixel's round mask sliced its white border off.
     Do not undo the inset in the script; the number and its two reference points (Chrome's
     WebAPK icon, Material's 57dp baseline) are in that script's docstring.

     The foreground is opaque rather than cut out to alpha: the glyph has a soft shadow over
     the beige and any alpha extraction leaves a halo. The background layer is that same
     beige, so the seam is invisible whatever mask the launcher applies.

     No <monochrome>: an opaque foreground would themed-render as a solid block. Without
     the tag Android 13+ falls back to this icon as drawn, which is the right picture. -->
<adaptive-icon xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">
  <background android:drawable="@color/ic_launcher_background"/>
  <foreground android:drawable="@mipmap/ic_launcher_foreground"/>
</adaptive-icon>
